IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `pt56-nouns`

-- Row il-56. THE FOUR ITEMS, as typed values, each with a sound control beside it.
-- A date printed twice is encoded as the Hebrew date and the Gregorian date on one line, with the day the Hebrew-calendar routine gives for the Hebrew date; a test compares the printed Gregorian date with the routine's day.
-- A reference is encoded as TWO descriptions: what the citing words say they point at, and what the target is, with a count of what is named and what the target has. Where they differ, the citation does not point at what it says.
-- An indexation clause is encoded as what it says about who publishes the updated amounts and about which amounts a claim for earlier damage uses.
-- A power to change a rate is encoded with the instrument made under it: the kind of instrument each names, the body whose approval each recites, and the dates.
-- DEFECTS: I1 (a signature line), I2 (a deeming sentence), I3 (an indexation clause), I4 (a rate power and the instrument made under it). Their failing assertions are in pt56-tests-xrefs-red.l4.
-- CONTROLS: the footer line of the page that carries I1; the sibling subsection that cites paragraph (7) of subsection (ב) (beside I2); the two citations of the indexation clause itself (beside I3); the approval the instrument recites and its 2025 rate (beside I4).
-- LIMBS. Limb (a) is the question whether the target is as the citing words say; limb (b) is the question whether the citing words read so in the instrument that made them.
-- Each limb says where it was read from (see `Where a reading comes from` in pt56-nouns.l4). The Hebrew Wikisource text is not official, and anything resting only on it is CONSOLIDATION-ONLY.
-- A date for which the sources give no answer is NOTHING; there is no REFUSE.

§ `Dates printed twice`

GIVEN d IS A `A date printed on a line`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the two dates on the line name one day` MEANS
    (d's `the Gregorian date printed`) EQUALS (d's `the day the Hebrew date falls on, by the routine`)

GIVEN d IS A `A date printed on a line`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days from the day by the routine to the Gregorian date printed` MEANS
    (Day (d's `the Gregorian date printed`)) - (Day (d's `the day the Hebrew date falls on, by the routine`))

-- THE ROUTINE. The Hebrew-calendar routine (Calendrical Calculations month numbering) was run first on its control date, 30 Kislev 5785, and gave 31 December 2024; the same routine then gave
-- 1 Av 5784 = 5 August 2024, 11 Av 5784 = 15 August 2024, 14 Av 5784 = 18 August 2024, 26 Adar I 5784 = 6 March 2024 and 27 Adar I 5784 = 7 March 2024 (5784 is a leap year in that routine).
